Changing only the registration increased the number of clear removals recognized after camera motion in both engines. On the tabletop, this reproduced the direction of the exploratory result with two different target objects. Yet the new shelf errors expose what a successful registration leaves unresolved. The map can align a panel over the remembered place. The appearance rule can then read a poor object match and a low obstruction score as evidence for removal. On the certified MuJoCo pair, a more accurate guess about the target state was impossible from the delivered input.

For a memory system with a cost for deleting a retained object, the clear-removal gain alone gives an incomplete basis for choosing a verifier. Each method's operating point has two consequences here. It determines how often the system makes a useful update when a change is visible and how often it commits when physical alternatives give it exactly the same evidence. The false removal with the blocker parked also shows why an audit needs to retain ordinary clear controls even when its main scientific question concerns occlusion.

The registered language model illustrates a second distinction. Its perfect screen on disjoint controls carried over to high clear-removal resolution, while false removals appeared on both new targets and in both engines. Nearly every UNKNOWN came from the upstream geometry check. When that check admitted a certified pair, the language model committed every time. This finding concerns the tested zero-shot adaptation and its fixed prompt. A trained registered-place verifier could be evaluated on the accompanying images and scored against the same counterfactual pairs.
